'''{{ill|Kaijin Shrine|ja| 海神神社|zh| 海神神社|simple}}''' (海神神社) also read as Watatsumi Shrine is a significant Shinto shrine located in [[Tsushima Island]], Nagasaki that holds a [[Beppyo shrine|Beppyo status]]. This status denotes that the shrine is remarkable and holds a significant historical importance. Kaijin Shrine is also ranked as an [[Ichinomiya]], which means it is the highest-ranked shrine in its province.<ref name="tsushima-island-2023a">{{Cite web |title=Mine Town |url=http://tsushima.weebly.com/mine-town.html |access-date=2023-11-20 |website=Tsushima Island |language=en}}</ref><ref name="rambelli-2018a">{{Cite book |last=Rambelli |first=F |title=The Sea and The Sacred in Japan |publisher=Bloomsbury Academic Publishing |year=2018 |isbn=978-1350062870 |location=Camden |pages=preface}}</ref>{{Rp|page=98}} It is located near Mount Izu.<ref>{{Cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=yCpQAAAAMAAJ&q=%22Kaijin+Shrine%22 |title=Proceedings |date=2006 |publisher=Architectural Institute of Korea |language=en}}</ref>

In Tsushima folklore it is said that [[Toyotama-hime]] and [[Hikohohodemi no Mikoto]] married at this shrine, and it is thus the sea god's palace.<ref name="faure-2021a" />{{rp||pages=|page=443}}

[[Empress Jingu]] visited the area to worship at Mount Izu.<ref name="tsushima-island-2023a" />

The shrine has a prominent set of Torii that are often compared to [[Itsukushima Shrine]] in how they enter water.<ref name="faure-2021a">{{Cite book |last=FAURE |first=BERNARD |url=http://dx.doi.org/10.2307/j.ctv1k3np30 |title=Rage and Ravage |date=2021-12-31 |publisher=University of Hawaii Press |isbn=978-0-8248-8936-4 |doi=10.2307/j.ctv1k3np30}}</ref>{{rp||pages=297}} It has five torii in a row with two in the ocean.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2013-09-28 |title=Tsushima: a boundary island of Japan {{!}} The Japan Times |url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130928050316/https://www.japantimes.co.jp/life/2013/09/21/travel/tsushima-a-boundary-island-of-japan/ |access-date=2023-11-20 |website=web.archive.org}}</ref> Recent archaeological findings suggest it is one of the oldest shrines in Japan, and [[Itsukushima Shrine]] was based on it.<ref name="faure-2021a" />{{rp||pages=321}}

'''[[ja:海神神社]]'''

The shrine is associated with [[Kamikaze (typhoon)|Kamikaze]], the typhoon that stopped the [[Mongol invasion of Japan|Mongol invasion of Japan.]]<ref name="rambelli-2018a" />[[Mongol invasion of Japan|{{Rp|page=97-99}}]] 

It has a Bronze [[Buddha statue]] from Korea in it.<ref>{{Cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=lWLrAAAAMAAJ&q=%22Kaijin+Shrine%22 |title=Archives of Asian Art |date=1987 |publisher=Asia Society |language=en}}</ref><ref name="php-international-2007a">{{Cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=lf0KAQAAMAAJ&q=%22Kaijin+Shrine%22 |title=Japan Close-up |date=2007 |publisher=PHP International |language=en}}</ref> The statue is designated as important cultural property.<ref name="php-international-2007a" />

In 2012 a robber stole a Silla era Buddha statue from the shrine<ref name="www2buddhistdoornet-2023a" /><ref name="faure-2021a" />{{rp||pages=321}}, on the same day they stole another statue from [[Kannon-ji]]<ref name="haeyeon-2023a">{{Cite web |last=Hae-yeon |first=Kim |date=2023-10-26 |title=Supreme Court dismisses Buseoksa's appeal in Bodhisattva statue ownership dispute |url=https://www.koreaherald.com/view.php?ud=20231026000667 |access-date=2023-10-27 |website=The Korea Herald |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Hae-yeon |first=Kim |date=2023-02-06 |title=Buseoksa to appeal court ruling on Bodhisattva statue stolen from Japan |url=https://www.koreaherald.com/view.php?ud=20230206000649 |access-date=2023-10-27 |website=The Korea Herald |language=en}}</ref> but in 2015 it was returned.<ref name="www2buddhistdoornet-2023a">{{Cite web |title=South Korea Returns Stolen Buddhist Statue to Japan {{!}} Buddhistdoor |url=https://www2.buddhistdoor.net/news/south-korea-returns-stolen-buddhist-statue-to-japan |access-date=2023-04-25 |website=www2.buddhistdoor.net}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Press |first=Jiji |date=2023-02-02 |title=S. Korean Court Denies Temple's Ownership of Statue Stolen from Japan |url=https://japannews.yomiuri.co.jp/society/general-news/20230202-88365/ |access-date=2023-10-27 |website=japannews.yomiuri.co.jp |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2015-05-27 |title=S. Korea court rejects suit seeking return of statue to Japan {{!}} GlobalPost |url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150527150211/http://www.globalpost.com/dispatch/news/kyodo-news-international/140502/s-korea-court-rejects-suit-seeking-return-statue-japan |access-date=2023-11-20 |website=web.archive.org}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2015-05-27 |title=S. Korea court rejects suit seeking return of statue to Japan {{!}} GlobalPost |url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150527150211/http://www.globalpost.com/dispatch/news/kyodo-news-international/140502/s-korea-court-rejects-suit-seeking-return-statue-japan |access-date=2023-11-20 |website=web.archive.org}}</ref><ref name="faure-2021a" />{{rp||pages=321}}

In January 2020 they banned foreigners due to behavioral issues by [[South Korea|South Korean]] guests.<ref name="webarchiveorg-2020a">{{Cite web |date=2020-01-14 |title=Japan’s Watatsumi Shrine bans foreigners after vandalism and alleged death threat against priest {{!}} South China Morning Post |url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200114080417/https://www.scmp.com/news/asia/east-asia/article/3046010/japans-watatsumi-shrine-bans-foreigners-after-vandalism-and |access-date=2023-11-20 |website=web.archive.org}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Wen |first=Lok Jian |date=2023-07-10 |title=Vandalism, peeling ceiling art: Japan temples suffer damage |language=en |work=The Straits Times |url=https://www.straitstimes.com/asia/east-asia/vandalism-peeling-ceiling-art-japan-temples-suffer-damage-to-tourist-sites#:~:text=In%20western%20Japan's%20Nagasaki%20prefecture,language%20and%20theft%20of%20amulets. |access-date=2023-11-20 |issn=0585-3923}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2020-01-13 |title=Japanese shrine bans foreign visitors following disrespectful behaviour by tourists |url=https://soranews24.com/2020/01/13/japanese-shrine-bans-foreign-visitors-following-disrespectful-behaviour-by-tourists/ |access-date=2023-11-20 |website=SoraNews24 -Japan News- |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Alyse |date=2023-02-09 |title=Overtourism in Japan: A Victim of its Own Success? |url=https://www.theinvisibletourist.com/overtourism-in-japan-tourist-pollution/ |access-date=2023-11-20 |website=The Invisible Tourist |language=en-AU}}</ref>
[[File:海神神社の空中写真.jpg|thumb|Shrine area]]
In 2020 it was damaged by a typhoon and [[Ghost of Tsushima]] fans helped raise money to repair it.<ref name="vgc-2021a">{{Cite web |date=2021-01-11 |title=Ghost of Tsushima fans have helped raise $260k for repairs on the real island |url=https://www.videogameschronicle.com/news/ghost-of-tsushima-fans-have-helped-raise-260k-for-repairs-on-the-real-island/ |access-date=2023-11-20 |website=VGC |language=en-GB}}</ref><ref name="destructoid-2021a">{{Cite web |date=2021-01-12 |title=Ghost of Tsushima fans come together to help fund repair of Watatsumi Shrine gate |url=https://www.destructoid.com/ghost-of-tsushima-fans-come-together-to-help-fund-repair-of-watatsumi-shrine-gate/ |access-date=2023-11-20 |website=Destructoid |language=en-US}}</ref><ref name="oloman-2021a">{{Cite web |last=Oloman |first=Jordan |date=2021-01-11 |title=Ghost of Tsushima Fans Help With Real-Life Tsushima Island Repairs |url=https://www.ign.com/articles/ghost-of-tsushima-fans-help-with-real-life-tsushima-island-repairs |access-date=2023-11-20 |website=IGN |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Kaijin Shrine Tathagata Buddha |url=https://research.cgu.edu/cultural-property-disputes-resource/cpdr/kaijin-shrine-tathagata-buddha |access-date=2023-11-20 |website=Cultural Property Disputes Resource |language=en-US}}</ref>

