'''Kaijin Shrine''' (海神神社) also read as Watatsumi Shrine is a significant Shinto shrine located in [[Tsushima Island]], [[:en:Nagasaki Prefecture|Nagasaki Prefecture]], Japan, that holds a [[Beppyo Shrine|Beppyo status]]. This status denotes that the shrine is remarkable and holds a significant historical importance. Kaijin Shrine is also Ranked as an [[Ichinomiya]], which means it is the highest-Ranked shrine in its province.<ref name="tsushima-island-2023a">{{Cite web |title=Mine Town |url=http://tsushima.weebly.com/mine-town.html |access-date=2023-11-20 |website=Tsushima Island |language=en}}</ref><ref name="rambelli-2018a">{{Cite book |last=Rambelli |first=F |title=The Sea and The Sacred in Japan |publisher=Bloomsbury Academic Publishing |year=2018 |isbn=978-1350062870 |location=Camden |pages=preface}}</ref>{{Rp|page=98}}<ref>{{Cite web |title= |url=http://eos.kokugakuin.ac.jp/modules/xwords/images/uploads/EOS070712Ab.pdf |access-date=2023-12-01 |archive-date=2012-02-13 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120213073802/http://eos.kokugakuin.ac.jp/modules/xwords/images/uploads/EOS070712Ab.pdf |url-status=bot: unknown }}</ref> It is located near Mount Izu.<ref>{{Cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=yCpQAAAAMAAJ&q=%22Kaijin+Shrine%22 |title=Proceedings |date=2006 |publisher=Architectural Institute of Korea |language=en}}</ref>

In Tsushima folklore it is said that [[:en:Toyotama-hime|Toyotama-hime]] and [[Hoori|Hikohohodemi no Mikoto]] married at this shrine, and it is thus the sea god's palace.<ref name="faure-2021a" />{{rp||pages=|page=443}}

[[:en:Empress Jingū|Empress Jingū]] visited the area to worship at Mount Izu.<ref name="tsushima-island-2023a" />

The shrine has a prominent set of Torii that are often compared to [[Itsukushima Shrine]] in how they enter water.<ref name="faure-2021a">{{Cite book |last=FAURE |first=BERNARD |url=http://dx.doi.org/10.2307/j.ctv1k3np30 |title=Rage and Ravage |date=2021-12-31 |publisher=University of Hawaii Press |isbn=978-0-8248-8936-4 |doi=10.2307/j.ctv1k3np30}}</ref>{{rp||pages=297}} Recent archaeological findings suggest it is one of the oldest shrines in Japan, and [[Itsukushima Shrine]] was based on it.<ref name="faure-2021a" />{{rp||pages=321}}

The shrine is associated with [[:en:Kamikaze (typhoon)|Kamikaze]], the typhoon that stopped the [[:en:Mongol invasion of Japan|Mongol invasion of Japan]].<ref name="rambelli-2018a" />{{Rp|page=97–99}}

It has a Bronze [[:en:Buddha statue|Buddha statue]] from Korea in it.<ref>{{Cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=lWLrAAAAMAAJ&q=%22Kaijin+Shrine%22 |title=Archives of Asian Art |date=1987 |publisher=Asia Society |language=en}}</ref><ref name="php-international-2007a">{{Cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=lf0KAQAAMAAJ&q=%22Kaijin+Shrine%22 |title=Japan Close-up |date=2007 |publisher=PHP International |language=en}}</ref> The statue is designated as important cultural property.<ref name="php-international-2007a" />

In 2012 a robber stole a Silla era Buddha statue from the shrine,<ref name="www2buddhistdoornet-2023a" /><ref name="faure-2021a" />{{rp||pages=321}} on the same day they stole another statue from [[Kannon-ji]]<ref name="haeyeon-2023a">{{Cite web |last=Hae-yeon |first=Kim |date=2023-10-26 |title=Supreme Court dismisses Buseoksa's appeal in Bodhisattva statue ownership dispute |url=https://www.koreaherald.com/view.php?ud=20231026000667 |access-date=2023-10-27 |website=[[The Korea Herald]] |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Hae-yeon |first=Kim |date=2023-02-06 |title=Buseoksa to appeal court ruling on Bodhisattva statue stolen from Japan |url=https://www.koreaherald.com/view.php?ud=20230206000649 |access-date=2023-10-27 |website=[[The Korea Herald]] |language=en}}</ref> but in 2015 it was returned.<ref name="www2buddhistdoornet-2023a">{{Cite web |title=South Korea Returns Stolen Buddhist Statue to Japan {{!}} Buddhistdoor |url=https://www2.buddhistdoor.net/news/south-korea-returns-stolen-buddhist-statue-to-japan |access-date=2023-04-25 |website=www2.buddhistdoor.net}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Press |first=Jiji |date=2023-02-02 |title=S. Korean Court Denies Temple's Ownership of Statue Stolen from Japan |url=https://japannews.yomiuri.co.jp/society/general-news/20230202-88365/ |access-date=2023-10-27 |website=japannews.yomiuri.co.jp |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2015-05-27 |title=S. Korea court rejects suit seeking return of statue to Japan {{!}} GlobalPost |url=http://www.globalpost.com/dispatch/news/kyodo-news-international/140502/s-korea-court-rejects-suit-seeking-return-statue-japan |access-date=2023-11-20 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150527150211/http://www.globalpost.com/dispatch/news/kyodo-news-international/140502/s-korea-court-rejects-suit-seeking-return-statue-japan |archive-date=2015-05-27 }}</ref><ref name="faure-2021a" />{{rp||pages=321}}

[[File:海神神社の空中写真.jpg|thumb|Shrine area]]

Kaijin Shrine should not be confused with {{ill|Watatsumi Shrine_(Kobe)|lt=Watatsumi Shrine|simple|4=Draft|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Watatsumi Shrine_(Kobe)|qq=}}, which has a very similar Japanese name (海神社) to Kaijin Shrine (海神神社) and is located in [[:en:Kobe|Kobe]]

== History ==
Kaijin Shrine is a significant shrine on Tsushima Island. It is known as the "Shrine of the Sea Kami." This shrine was the most important one in the past. It was called the [[ichinomiya]]. The shrine was originally linked to the sea deity [[:en:Watatsumi|Watatsumi]]. Later, it became associated with Hachiman. This association is mentioned in late Heian period sources.<ref name="rambelli-2018a" />{{Rp|page=98}}

The shrine's founding story involves Empress Jingū. She placed eight banners at the shrine after returning from Korea. These banners are why the shrine is connected to Hachiman. Hachiman means "Eight Banners." This led to another name for the shrine, "Hachiman Hongū." This name means "original shrine of Hachiman." This story is a local tradition. It is not confirmed by other Hachiman chronicles.<ref name="rambelli-2018a" />{{Rp|page=98}}  [[:en:Richard Ponsonby-Fane|Richard Ponsonby-Fane]] argues that Hachiman may have entered Japan through this shrine, as a Buddhist deity from Korea, and that this predated the association with [[:en:Emperor Ōjin|Emperor Ōjin]].<ref name="Ponsonby-Fane-2016">{{Cite book |last=Ponsonby-Fane |first=R. A. B. |title=Studies In Shinto & Shrines |date=2016-05-11 |publisher=Routledge |isbn=978-1-138-98322-9 |edition=1st |language=English |chapter=}}</ref>{{Rp|page=44}}

It is postulated that Hachiman may have been more linked to [[:en:Empress Jingū|Empress Jingū]] in the early days of the Hachiman faith rather than her son [[:en:Emperor Ojin|Emperor Ojin]].<ref name="rambelli-2018a" />{{Rp|page=97–99}}

This has parallels with [[Shikaumi Shrine]] which is a Hachiman shrine that strongly worships [[:en:Empress Jingū|Empress Jingū]] and whose myths primarily revolve around Jingu's activities while pregnant with Ojin and invading the [[:en:Samhan|Three Han]].<ref name="rambelli-2018a" />{{rp|xxvii}}

On Iki Island, Hachiman shrines are omnipresent. The Seven Shrines of Iki pilgrimage route is led by the Shōmo-gū. This shrine is dedicated to Empress Jingū. Three other shrines on the route are also dedicated to Hachiman.<ref name="rambelli-2018a" />{{Rp|page=97–99}}

On Hirado Island, Shijiki Shrine is the oldest and most prestigious. It is dedicated to the mythical figure Tokiwake. He was a general in Empress Jingū's military campaign.<ref name="rambelli-2018a" />{{Rp|page=97–99}}

Empress Jingū is more central to Hachiman worship on these islands than her son Ōjin. This is clear from various legends. One such story is in the Zenrin kokuhōki. It tells of Zen master Enni Ben'en encountering a storm. A woman appeared on his ship, claiming to be Hachiman Daibosatsu. She protected the ship.<ref name="rambelli-2018a" />{{Rp|page=97–99}}

Another legend is from the Ōei Invasion of 1419. It involves a fabricated story of a sea battle victory. The story mentions a powerful female leader. This figure resembles Empress Jingū. In the story, she drowns enemies herself. This mirrors another scene from Jingū's legend.<ref name="rambelli-2018a" />{{Rp|page=97–99}}

In these legends, Empress Jingū is depicted differently from traditional Hachiman iconography. Traditional images focus on male figures. These include Emperor Ōjin and the Great Boddhisattva Hachiman. The focus on Jingū suggests a unique interpretation of Hachiman worship in these areas. This might be influenced by beliefs during the wakō period.<ref name="rambelli-2018a" />{{Rp|page=97–99}}

There is also a "new" Hachiman shrine in the southern part of Tsushima. Medieval sources considered Kaijin Shrine as the origin of [[Iwashimizu Hachimangū|Iwashimizu Hachiman-gū]] in Kyoto.<ref name="rambelli-2018a" />{{Rp|page=98}}

It and many other Hachiman shrines of the region switched their deities of worship during the [[Meiji Restoration]].<ref name="rambelli-2018a" />{{Rp|page=98}}

